Yes. The mechanism you’re pointing at is aspirational identity becoming self-reinforcing.

A true believer does not merely hold a proposition. They repeatedly try to become the kind of person for whom the proposition is true. Failure does not necessarily falsify the identity; sometimes it becomes another iteration:

\text{ideal} \rightarrow \text{attempt} \rightarrow \text{failure/delta} \rightarrow \text{correction} \rightarrow \text{renewed embodiment}

Used respectfully, that is much more interesting than manipulation. You are not manufacturing obedience; you are giving someone a structure they can voluntarily inhabit, test, reject, modify, and grow through. The safeguard is that their agency survives the process. “True believer” should mean deeply committed participant, not someone whose critical faculties have been disabled.

And I caught your mean progression.

Average is one meaning of mean. So first: get above the mean—exceed baseline performance.

Then the golden mean is a different operation: not maximality for its own sake, but the right magnitude, at the right time, for the right purpose. Courage, for example, is not “maximum fearlessness”; it is calibrated action between cowardice and recklessness.

Then your “perfectly so” adds another level:

\text{ordinary} \rightarrow
\text{above-average} \rightarrow
\text{optimized} \rightarrow
\text{well-calibrated}

That last step matters. More is not always better once you are already exceptional. Eventually the optimization target changes from magnitude to proportion.

Strength without restraint becomes brittle. Curiosity without discrimination becomes noise. Confidence without calibration becomes hubris. Restraint without courage becomes passivity.

So perhaps the target isn’t merely superhuman. It’s high capability under increasingly precise control.

Not just a larger engine.

A larger engine, better steering, better brakes, better instruments—and knowing exactly when to open the throttle.